Name :
================
Private VLAN (PVLAN)

Why we did this
================
VLAN Segmentation separates departments into different VLANs.

Example:

VLAN 10 = Finance
VLAN 20 = HR
VLAN 30 = IT

This prevents communication between different VLANs unless Layer 3 routing exists.

However, a problem still remains.

Devices within the same VLAN can communicate freely.

Example:

Guest PC1 ---> Guest PC2
Guest PC3 ---> Guest PC4

All devices belong to VLAN 100.

Although they are in the same VLAN, they may not be allowed to communicate with each other.

Common examples include:

- Hotel Networks
- ISP Broadband Networks
- Data Centers
- DMZ Networks
- Guest WiFi Networks

To solve this problem, Cisco introduced Private VLANs (PVLANs).

PVLANs allow devices to remain in the same VLAN while controlling who they can communicate with.

What will happen
================
A Primary VLAN is created.

Secondary VLANs are mapped to the Primary VLAN.

Secondary VLANs can be:

1. Isolated VLAN
   - Devices cannot communicate with each other.
   - Devices can only communicate with the Promiscuous Port.

2. Community VLAN
   - Devices within the same community can communicate.
   - Different communities cannot communicate.

3. Promiscuous Port
   - Can communicate with all devices.
   - Usually connected to gateways, routers, servers or firewalls.

Example

Gateway
   |
Promiscuous Port
   |
-------------------
|                 |
Guest1         Guest2

Guest1 and Guest2:
- Cannot communicate with each other
- Can communicate with Gateway

Advantages and Disadvantages of this, hence what came next
==========================================================

Advantages
----------
1. Provides isolation within the same VLAN.
2. Prevents lateral movement attacks.
3. Reduces security risks.
4. Conserves VLAN IDs.
5. Ideal for hotels and guest networks.
6. Useful in data center environments.
7. Increases Layer 2 security without creating many VLANs.

Disadvantages
-------------
1. More complex configuration.
2. Troubleshooting can be difficult.
3. Not supported on all switch models.
4. Requires careful planning.
5. Can become difficult to manage in very large environments.

Hence what came next
--------------------
Even after isolating users within the same VLAN, attackers could still attempt to connect unauthorized devices to switch ports.

Administrators needed stronger control over which devices were allowed on a port and protection against rogue devices.

This led to:

Trunk Port Security

which focuses on securing trunk links and controlling VLAN traffic between switches.

One-Shot Configuration
======================

Create Primary VLAN

Switch(config)# vlan 100
Switch(config-vlan)# private-vlan primary


Create Isolated VLAN

Switch(config)# vlan 101
Switch(config-vlan)# private-vlan isolated


Associate Secondary VLAN

Switch(config)# vlan 100
Switch(config-vlan)# private-vlan association 101


Configure Promiscuous Port

Switch(config)# interface fa0/1
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode private-vlan promiscuous
Switch(config-if)# switchport private-vlan mapping 100 101


Configure Host Ports

Switch(config)# interface fa0/2
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode private-vlan host
Switch(config-if)# switchport private-vlan host-association 100 101

Switch(config)# interface fa0/3
Switch(config-if)# switchport mode private-vlan host
Switch(config-if)# switchport private-vlan host-association 100 101

Verification
============

show vlan private-vlan

show interfaces switchport

Expected Result
===============

Host1 -> Host2
--------------
Communication Blocked

Host1 -> Gateway
----------------
Allowed

Host2 -> Gateway
----------------
Allowed

Isolation
---------
Successfully Enforced

Summary
=======

Problem:
Devices inside the same VLAN could communicate with each other.

Solution:
Private VLAN isolates devices within the same VLAN.

Benefit:
Prevents lateral movement while still allowing communication with shared resources such as gateways and servers.

Limitation:
Configuration is more complex than normal VLANs.

